1
00:00:06,220 --> 00:00:11,290
A food company makes a tin 8 centimetres tall, with radius 3 centimetres.

2
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:17,170
It needs a paper label all round the side, and metal for the top and bottom.

3
00:00:17,220 --> 00:00:21,840
When you unroll the label, what shape is it: a rectangle, or a circle?

4
00:00:21,890 --> 00:00:25,040
Pause the video and choose.

5
00:00:25,090 --> 00:00:26,730
A rectangle.

6
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:29,450
Remember: a net folds into a 3D shape.

7
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:33,280
The surface area is the total area of the net.

8
00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:36,250
The rectangle's height is the tin's height.

9
00:00:36,300 --> 00:00:41,020
Its length goes all the way round: the circumference, 2 pi R.

10
00:00:41,070 --> 00:00:41,710
Your turn.

11
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:44,950
Find the area of the label, to 1 decimal place.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:48,210
Pause the video and try it.

13
00:00:48,260 --> 00:00:54,130
2 times pi times 3 times 8 is about 150.8 square centimetres.

14
00:00:54,180 --> 00:00:58,310
The top and bottom are two circles, each pi R squared.

15
00:00:58,360 --> 00:00:59,010
Your turn.

16
00:00:59,060 --> 00:01:03,140
Find the total surface area of the tin, to 1 decimal place.

17
00:01:03,190 --> 00:01:06,080
Pause and work it out.

18
00:01:06,130 --> 00:01:10,250
The two circles: 2 times pi times 9, about 56.5.

19
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:14,780
Add the label: about 207.3 square centimetres.

20
00:01:14,830 --> 00:01:18,520
A triangular prism has 2 triangles and 3 rectangles.

21
00:01:18,570 --> 00:01:21,030
A pyramid has a base and triangles.

22
00:01:21,080 --> 00:01:24,020
Sketch the net and list each face.

23
00:01:24,070 --> 00:01:25,410
Here is the method.

24
00:01:25,460 --> 00:01:27,650
Step 1: sketch the net.

25
00:01:27,700 --> 00:01:30,670
Step 2: find the area of each face.

26
00:01:30,720 --> 00:01:34,820
Step 3: check for open faces, like a cup with no lid.

27
00:01:34,870 --> 00:01:38,200
Step 4: add them up, and give the units.

28
00:01:38,250 --> 00:01:41,410
A cup with no lid: radius 4, height 9.

29
00:01:41,460 --> 00:01:44,780
One circle, 16 pi, plus the side, 72 pi.

30
00:01:44,830 --> 00:01:49,210
That is 88 pi, about 276.5 square centimetres.

31
00:01:49,260 --> 00:01:53,920
A square-based pyramid: base 10, slant height 13.

32
00:01:53,970 --> 00:01:55,460
The base is 100.

33
00:01:55,510 --> 00:02:00,750
Each triangle is a half times 10 times 13, which is 65.

34
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:03,890
100 plus 4 times 65 is 360.

35
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:07,920
Amy finds the label for a tin with radius 3 and height 8.

36
00:02:07,970 --> 00:02:09,880
Which line is wrong?

37
00:02:09,930 --> 00:02:11,020
Line 1 is wrong.

38
00:02:11,070 --> 00:02:13,450
The label wraps all the way round.

39
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:16,290
Its length is the circumference.

40
00:02:16,340 --> 00:02:19,310
2 pi times 3 is about 18.85.

41
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:22,330
Times 8 is about 150.8.

42
00:02:22,380 --> 00:02:30,350
Can makers choose the radius and height carefully, to hold the same amount using as little metal as possible.

43
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:31,780
One last question.

44
00:02:31,830 --> 00:02:34,730
A cylinder has radius 5 and height 6.

45
00:02:34,780 --> 00:02:37,680
Is its surface area 110 pi, or 80 pi?

46
00:02:37,730 --> 00:02:41,240
Pause the video and choose.

47
00:02:41,290 --> 00:02:43,500
2 pi R squared is 50 pi.

48
00:02:43,550 --> 00:02:45,200
2 pi R H is 60 pi.

49
00:02:45,250 --> 00:02:47,360
Together, 110 pi.

50
00:02:47,410 --> 00:02:52,610
To remember: a cylinder's surface is 2 pi R squared plus 2 pi R H.

51
00:02:52,660 --> 00:02:55,340
The curved surface is 2 pi R H.

52
00:02:55,390 --> 00:02:57,680
Prisms: two ends plus rectangles.

53
00:02:57,730 --> 00:03:00,500
Pyramids: a base plus triangles.

54
00:03:00,550 --> 00:03:03,010
Check for open faces.

55
00:03:03,060 --> 00:03:08,390
Great work! Now practise lesson 14.2 on Camday.
